/*
PROBLEM: FERRIS WHEEL
=================================================

APPROACH: GREEDY APPROACH + SORTING + TWO POINTERS

PROBLEM UNDERSTANDING:
-------------------------------------------------
We are given:
1. n children.
2. An array arr[], where arr[i] represents the weight of the i-th child.
3. max_weight (x), which represents the maximum weight allowed in one gondola.

Our task is to find the MINIMUM number of gondolas required
to take all the children on the Ferris wheel.

IMPORTANT RULES:
1. Each gondola can carry maximum 2 children.
2. The total weight of children in one gondola must not
   exceed max_weight.
3. Every child must get a gondola.
4. A child cannot be divided into multiple gondolas.

-------------------------------------------------
GREEDY APPROACH:
-------------------------------------------------

The main idea is to always consider the HEAVIEST child first.

We sort the array in ascending order.

After sorting:
- arr[i] represents the lightest child.
- arr[j] represents the heaviest child.

We use two pointers:

i = 0       -> points to the lightest child
j = n - 1   -> points to the heaviest child

We try to put the lightest and heaviest children together.

CASE 1:
If arr[i] + arr[j] <= max_weight:

Both children can fit in one gondola.

Therefore:
- Increase i to move to the next lightest child.
- Decrease j to move to the next heaviest child.
- Increase ans because one gondola has been used.

CASE 2:
If arr[i] + arr[j] > max_weight:

Both children cannot fit together.

Since arr[j] is the heaviest child, we cannot pair
this child with any other remaining child.

Therefore:
- Put the heaviest child alone.
- Decrease j.
- Increase ans.

IMPORTANT:
We do not need to check other children for the heaviest child.

If the heaviest child cannot fit with the lightest child,
then it cannot fit with any other remaining child because
all other children are equally heavy or heavier than arr[i].

-------------------------------------------------
WHY DO WE USE SORTING?
-------------------------------------------------

Sorting helps us easily find:

1. The lightest child using arr[i].
2. The heaviest child using arr[j].

This allows us to decide whether both children can
fit in the same gondola.

-------------------------------------------------
WHY DO WE USE TWO POINTERS?
-------------------------------------------------

We use two pointers because we need to consider
the lightest and heaviest remaining children.

i moves from the beginning.
j moves from the end.

When both children are paired:
i++;
j--;

When the heaviest child goes alone:
j--;

This continues until all children are assigned.

-------------------------------------------------
WHY IS WHILE(i <= j) USED?
-------------------------------------------------

The loop continues while i <= j.

This handles two situations:

1. i < j:
   There are at least two remaining children.
   We check whether they can fit together.

2. i == j:
   Only one child remains.
   This child needs one separate gondola.

In your code, you handle the second case using:

if(i == j)
{
    return ans + 1;
}

This is correct because one child remains,
and that child needs one more gondola.

-------------------------------------------------
EXAMPLE:
-------------------------------------------------

Input:
n = 4
max_weight = 10

arr[] = {7, 2, 3, 9}

STEP 1:
Sort the array.

arr[] = {2, 3, 7, 9}

i = 0
j = 3
ans = 0

STEP 2:
Lightest child = arr[0] = 2
Heaviest child = arr[3] = 9

2 + 9 = 11

11 > 10

Both cannot fit together.

Put the heaviest child (9) alone.

j = 2
ans = 1

STEP 3:
Lightest child = arr[0] = 2
Heaviest child = arr[2] = 7

2 + 7 = 9

9 <= 10

Both can fit together.

Put 2 and 7 in one gondola.

i = 1
j = 1
ans = 2

STEP 4:
Now i == j.

Only one child remains.

arr[1] = 3

This child needs one gondola.

Return ans + 1.

Answer = 3 gondolas.

-------------------------------------------------
TIME COMPLEXITY:
-------------------------------------------------

Sorting:
O(n log n)

Two pointer traversal:
O(n)

Total:
O(n log n)

-------------------------------------------------
AUXILIARY SPACE COMPLEXITY:
-------------------------------------------------

Sorting is done directly on the original array.

Two pointers and ans use constant extra space.

Auxiliary space:
O(1) excluding sorting implementation details.

=================================================
CODE WITH COMMENTS
=================================================
*/

#include <iostream>
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

// Function to calculate the minimum number of gondolas.
//
// arr[]       -> Array containing the weight of each child.
// max_weight  -> Maximum weight allowed in one gondola.
// n           -> Total number of children.
//
// We use int &n to pass n by reference.
// However, n does not need to be changed inside this function.
// It can simply be passed as int n.
int f(int arr[], int max_weight, int &n)
{
    // ans stores the total number of gondolas required.
    int ans = 0;

    // Sort the array in ascending order.
    //
    // After sorting:
    // arr[0]     -> Lightest child.
    // arr[n - 1] -> Heaviest child.
    sort(arr, arr + n);

    // i points to the lightest remaining child.
    int i = 0;

    // j points to the heaviest remaining child.
    int j = n - 1;

    // Continue until all children are assigned.
    //
    // i <= j also handles the case when only
    // one child remains.
    while (i <= j)
    {
        // CASE 1:
        // Only one child remains.
        //
        // Since i == j, both pointers point to
        // the same child.
        //
        // This child needs one separate gondola.
        if (i == j)
        {
            // Return the current gondola count + 1.
            return ans + 1;
        }

        // Store the weight of the lightest remaining child.
        int left = arr[i];

        // Store the weight of the heaviest remaining child.
        int right = arr[j];

        // CASE 2:
        // Check whether the lightest and heaviest children
        // can fit together in one gondola.
        if (left + right <= max_weight)
        {
            // Both children can fit together.

            // Move i to the next lightest child.
            i++;

            // Move j to the next heaviest child.
            j--;

            // One gondola has been used for these two children.
            ans++;
        }
        else
        {
            // CASE 3:
            // The lightest and heaviest children cannot fit
            // together because their total weight exceeds
            // max_weight.
            //
            // Since arr[j] is the heaviest remaining child,
            // it cannot fit with any other remaining child.
            //
            // Therefore, put the heaviest child alone.

            // One gondola has been used.
            ans++;

            // Remove the heaviest child from consideration.
            j--;
        }
    }

    // Return the total number of gondolas.
    //
    // Normally, the function returns from the i == j case
    // when one child remains.
    //
    // If all children have already been assigned,
    // return ans.
    return ans;
}

int main()
{
    // n represents the total number of children.
    // x represents the maximum weight allowed
    // in one gondola.
    int n, x;

    // Read the number of children and maximum weight.
    cin >> n >> x;

    // Create an array to store the weight of each child.
    //
    // NOTE:
    // int arr[n] is supported by some compilers like GCC,
    // but it is not standard C++.
    int arr[n];

    // Read the weight of every child.
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        // Store the weight of the current child.
        int ele;

        // Read the child's weight.
        cin >> ele;

        // Store the weight in the array.
        arr[i] = ele;
    }

    // Call the function.
    //
    // f(arr, x, n):
    // arr -> Array containing children's weights.
    // x   -> Maximum weight allowed in one gondola.
    // n   -> Total number of children.
    //
    // Print the minimum number of gondolas required.
    cout << f(arr, x, n) << endl;

    return 0;
}
